import{_ as t,o,c as s,ao as a}from"./chunks/framework.DlFQC2ob.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Design decisions","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"explanation/design-decisions.md","filePath":"explanation/design-decisions.md","lastUpdated":null}'),n={name:"explanation/design-decisions.md"};function r(i,e,d,h,c,l){return o(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="Design-decisions" tabindex="-1">Design decisions <a class="header-anchor" href="#Design-decisions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Design decisions {#Design-decisions}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The decisions that shape FFTL, each with the reason and the evidence it rests on. A decision stays here until something replaces it; a replaced decision is marked, so a reader who finds the old reasoning elsewhere can see that it no longer holds. Numbers are measurements on an Apple M1 Max and on AMD EPYC (Zen 2) unless a line says otherwise.</p><h2 id="Licence-and-provenance" tabindex="-1">Licence and provenance <a class="header-anchor" href="#Licence-and-provenance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Licence and provenance {#Licence-and-provenance}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> FFTL is MIT-licensed. No GPL code is read, copied, translated or closely paraphrased. FFTW is a benchmark competitor only. Concepts known from the literature are used; code structure, naming and style deliberately do not follow FFTW (a 25-year-old C library with generated codelets and a measuring planner). Permissive sources may be read; a close take-over keeps its notice. The rules for contributors are on the <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR28/howto/contribute#Contribute">Contribute</a> page and in <code>AGENTS.md</code>.</p><h2 id="Codelets-with-complex-values-in-SIMD-registers" tabindex="-1">Codelets with complex values in SIMD registers <a class="header-anchor" href="#Codelets-with-complex-values-in-SIMD-registers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Codelets with complex values in SIMD registers {#Codelets-with-complex-values-in-SIMD-registers}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> A codelet works on complex values interleaved in SIMD registers. On aarch64 with the ARMv8.3 complex-number extension it uses <code>FCADD</code> and <code>FCMLA</code>, elsewhere shuffles (x86: <code>vfmaddsub</code> for products, <code>addsub</code> for ±i). Whether the extension is present is decided when the package is compiled: Apple silicon always, Linux by the <code>fcma</code> flag in <code>/proc/cpuinfo</code> (LLVM&#39;s host feature query returns nothing on macOS).</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> On the M1 this layout with <code>FCADD</code>/<code>FCMLA</code> was the first variant that beat FFTW; keeping split real and imaginary parts in registers spilled for <code>Float64</code>. The layout is a per-platform choice behind dispatch on the vector type, so the generator and the plan structure stay shared.</p><h2 id="A-heuristic-radix-choice,-no-measuring-planner" tabindex="-1">A heuristic radix choice, no measuring planner <a class="header-anchor" href="#A-heuristic-radix-choice,-no-measuring-planner" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A heuristic radix choice, no measuring planner {#A-heuristic-radix-choice,-no-measuring-planner}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> The radix sequence of a length comes from a fixed rule on its prime factorisation (<code>src/factor.jl</code>). There is no measuring planner by default.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> Brute-force searches over all decompositions put the best and the second-best within 1–3 %, and the large losses of an estimate-only planner are cache effects the algorithm has to avoid anyway. A per-machine calibration or an optional measuring mode can follow if the rule leaves too much on the table.</p><h2 id="Rules-for-lengths-are-derived-on-some-lengths-and-checked-on-others" tabindex="-1">Rules for lengths are derived on some lengths and checked on others <a class="header-anchor" href="#Rules-for-lengths-are-derived-on-some-lengths-and-checked-on-others" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rules for lengths are derived on some lengths and checked on others {#Rules-for-lengths-are-derived-on-some-lengths-and-checked-on-others}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-04).</strong> The radix choice and the Bluestein length are rules on the prime factorisation, not tables per length. A rule is derived from one part of the lengths (a brute-force search over all decompositions, or a cost model) and checked on lengths that were not used for the derivation, for example by parity of n or by a random split; only the checked result counts as evidence. A mechanism with a stated cause (a cost estimate) is preferred over a list of lengths. Rules are per architecture where the search differs. After every change of a rule, the dense sweep is repeated and its distribution (median, share above 1.2×, 1.5×, 2×, worst lengths) is the criterion, not single points.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> The first rules were fitted to a few dozen lengths, mostly powers of two and smooth lengths on the M1. A dense sweep of every length 2…1024 against FFTW MEASURE (one thread, Zen 2, 2046 cases) showed 100 <code>Float32</code> and 64 <code>Float64</code> lengths slower than 1.5×, the worst at 3.4×.</p><h2 id="Lengths-with-large-prime-factors:-split-off-the-smooth-part,-Bluestein-on-the-rest" tabindex="-1">Lengths with large prime factors: split off the smooth part, Bluestein on the rest <a class="header-anchor" href="#Lengths-with-large-prime-factors:-split-off-the-smooth-part,-Bluestein-on-the-rest" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Lengths with large prime factors: split off the smooth part, Bluestein on the rest {#Lengths-with-large-prime-factors:-split-off-the-smooth-part,-Bluestein-on-the-rest}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-07).</strong> For n = s·q, with q the part made of primes above 31: one Cooley–Tukey step, a chain of length s, twiddles, and s Bluestein transforms of length q (<code>Composite</code>). A leftover q stays one Bluestein while its convolution is at most 4 MiB; above that, q is split into its prime powers, recursively. Bluestein convolutions of 8 MiB and more run as a serial four-step algorithm on x86 (1.3× from 2²⁰ on, Zen 2) but not on aarch64, where the chain is faster up to 2²² (M1 Max). With threads, <code>CompositeMT</code> is used when s is at least the number of threads and is bitwise equal to the serial kernel; otherwise the whole length is one <code>BluesteinMT</code>.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> The convolutions of length about 2q stay in cache, and the arithmetic equals one Bluestein of length n. On 45 non-smooth lengths (Zen 2) FFTL moved from ×0.64 to ×1.04 of the fastest other library with one thread, and from ×0.77 to ×1.16 with 16 threads.</p><p><strong>Not done / not checked.</strong> Rader&#39;s algorithm (5 of the 45 sweep lengths qualify). Both thresholds were measured on Zen 2 only, and the 4 MiB rule is checked on 12 lengths that also took part in its derivation.</p><h2 id="Two-chain-forms:-compiled-for-short-smooth-lengths,-run-time-data-for-the-rest" tabindex="-1">Two chain forms: compiled for short smooth lengths, run-time data for the rest <a class="header-anchor" href="#Two-chain-forms:-compiled-for-short-smooth-lengths,-run-time-data-for-the-rest" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two chain forms: compiled for short smooth lengths, run-time data for the rest {#Two-chain-forms:-compiled-for-short-smooth-lengths,-run-time-data-for-the-rest}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-09).</strong> <code>kernel</code> builds the compiled <code>Chain</code> (radix sequence in the type) for smooth lengths n ≤ 128 (<code>SMALL_N</code>) and the <code>RChain</code> (radix sequence as data, a fixed set of codelets) for every other smooth length. The choice is made when the plan is built, so a call carries no dispatch. Both forms share the twiddle tables, the radix rule and the vector-type rules (bitwise equal in 552 checks). <code>RChain</code> is also the only form the trimmed C library can contain.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> A plan of a new size with <code>Chain</code> is compilation, not planning: 0.31–0.48 s for plan and first call against 0.013–0.044 s with the run-time plan (M1, one fresh process per case). But on x86 the run-time choice of the codelet costs 9–20 ns per call (n = 16, <code>Float64</code>: <code>Chain</code> 22 ns, <code>RChain</code> 39 ns), which is 1.2–1.5× for short lines. With the split, short lengths are at parity or better. Warm execution of the run-time plan against the compiled one was 1.00–1.08 on the cases measured, with one exception (Bluestein, 100003, <code>Float32</code>: 1.42, cause open).</p><p><strong>Replaced.</strong> The idea of removing <code>Chain</code> altogether (2026-11-08) and the earlier decision to keep the JIT and run-time plan families as separate types (2026-11-02).</p><h2 id="Kernels-of-a-fixed-set-are-plain-methods,-not-@generated" tabindex="-1">Kernels of a fixed set are plain methods, not <code>@generated</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#Kernels-of-a-fixed-set-are-plain-methods,-not-@generated" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Kernels of a fixed set are plain methods, not `@generated` {#Kernels-of-a-fixed-set-are-plain-methods,-not-@generated}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-10).</strong> The stage kernels of the codelet set that <code>RChain</code> dispatches to, and the compiled chains of the short lengths, are plain methods defined in <code>src/kernel_methods.jl</code> from the bodies the <code>@generated</code> methods build. The <code>@generated</code> methods remain as the fallback.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> A package image keeps the complete optimised IR of every precompiled <code>@generated</code> specialisation, 130 MB of the 146 MB image. See <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR28/explanation/image-size#Package-size-and-load-time">Package size and load time</a>.</p><h2 id="The-first-stage-of-a-single-line-runs-at-full-vector-width" tabindex="-1">The first stage of a single line runs at full vector width <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-first-stage-of-a-single-line-runs-at-full-vector-width" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The first stage of a single line runs at full vector width {#The-first-stage-of-a-single-line-runs-at-full-vector-width}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-12).</strong> The first stage of a single line (stride 1, power-of-two radix, not the last stage) runs <code>stage_small!</code> where the widest vector holds more complex values than the grouped first stage handles (C = 8, <code>Float32</code> on AVX-512): it is vectorised over the butterflies p, so every input is one contiguous load, the twiddles come from the grouped first-stage table, and the R outputs are interleaved in registers into contiguous stores. The generator checks the permutation of the interleave. Both chain forms use it; the kernels (radices 2, 4, 8, 16 × sign, widest vector only) are part of the fixed set of plain methods.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> That stage ran the grouped first stage on 4 complex values. <code>Float64</code> has no such stage on AVX-512. Intel Core i7-1185G7, one line, <code>Float32</code>, <code>RChain</code>, radix sequences of 16s and 8s, µs without → with: 128: 0.093 → 0.069, 256: 0.176 → 0.131, 1024: 0.81 → 0.61, 2048: 1.60 → 1.35, 4096: 3.75 → 3.29, 8192: 7.85 → 6.69, 16384: 18.0 → 15.4 (the same code against itself differs by up to 3 %).</p><p><strong>Not done.</strong> The same scheme for later stages with a stride s = 2 or 4 below C (vectorised over q + s·p). It needs a per-lane twiddle table for every such stage, one kernel per stride and a stride switch in the <code>RChain</code> dispatch. Only <code>Float32</code> lengths whose radices start with 4, 4 have such a stage (s = 4; 625 lengths up to 70000, 65536 the only power of two), none with s = 2. AMD EPYC 9354, one line, <code>Float32</code>, <code>RChain</code>, time without the s = 4 stage over time with it, medians of 10 interleaved rounds: 192: 1.08, 320: 1.06, 768: 1.03, 960: 1.03, 3072: 0.96, 4800: 0.99, 20480: 0.97; 65536: 0.96 and 262144: 0.97 from 5 rounds. The same code against itself differs by 5–20 % between rounds on that machine, so only the 6–8 % at 192 and 320 (the same in a second series) stands out.</p><p><strong>Not done.</strong> Passing m and s to the stage kernels as constants of a single line (immediates for the stream offsets) needs one kernel per length, which only the compiled <code>Chain</code> can have. Measured in <code>Chain</code> against the same kernels with run-time m and s, lengths 16–16384, both element types: ratios 0.90–1.04 without a trend, inside the spread of the measurement. Not verified on other machines.</p><h2 id="Stage-kernels-keep-twiddles-in-memory-and-address-streams-from-few-registers" tabindex="-1">Stage kernels keep twiddles in memory and address streams from few registers <a class="header-anchor" href="#Stage-kernels-keep-twiddles-in-memory-and-address-streams-from-few-registers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stage kernels keep twiddles in memory and address streams from few registers {#Stage-kernels-keep-twiddles-in-memory-and-address-streams-from-few-registers}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-12).</strong> The stages vectorised over q (<code>stage!</code>, <code>stage_range!</code>, <code>stage_last!</code>) are written so that the inner loop needs few registers besides the data:</p><ul><li><p>The twiddle multiply reads re(w) and im(w) from the table as scalar broadcasts, a·w = (a·re(w)) ∓ (swap(a)·im(w)) with the sign alternating over the lanes (<code>ccmul_at</code>). On x86 both become memory operands of the multiply and the fused multiply. Every complex multiply outside the FCMA path has this form (<code>ccmul_parts</code>), so all kernels round alike and threaded and single-threaded transforms stay bit-identical.</p></li><li><p>The R input and R output streams are addressed as base + odd·stride·2^e with odd in (1, 3, 5, 7) and one base per 8 streams (<code>stream_addr</code>). The base pointer of the loop passes through <code>llvm.launder.invariant.group</code> and the scaling is a typed GEP: without both, LLVM&#39;s loop strength reduction rewrites the addresses into one hoisted pointer per stream.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Why.</strong> A radix-16 stage has 15 twiddles, 16 input and 16 output streams. With the twiddles (and their swapped copies) in registers and one pointer per stream, the inner loop reloaded vectors and pointers from the stack. Instructions per butterfly in the inner loop, AMD EPYC 9354 (AVX-512), <code>Float64</code> (<code>Float32</code> within 3), before → after: radix 16 middle stage 284 → 198, last stage 182 → 151; radix 8 middle stage 100 → 79, last stage 71 → 58.</p><p>Time: the twiddle multiply alone (in an earlier form written with the x86 <code>vfmaddsub</code> intrinsics), one line, <code>plan_fft</code>, medians of 8 interleaved rounds, time after over time before: <code>Float32</code> 512–16384: 0.88–0.93 (8192: 0.94 on the minima), <code>Float64</code> 1024, 4096, 8192, 16384: 0.94–0.96, lengths of 32768 and above 0.98–1.01; the same code against itself differs by 1–5 % for most lengths. The time of both changes together is not verified, and neither change is verified on aarch64.</p><h2 id="Codelets-of-the-radices-17–31-are-compiled-on-first-use" tabindex="-1">Codelets of the radices 17–31 are compiled on first use <a class="header-anchor" href="#Codelets-of-the-radices-17–31-are-compiled-on-first-use" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Codelets of the radices 17–31 are compiled on first use {#Codelets-of-the-radices-17–31-are-compiled-on-first-use}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-10).</strong> The codelets of the radices 17, 19, 23, 29 and 31 are not part of the package image. The first call compiles them (70–100 ms); later calls go through a table of callable objects at a cost of about 0.02 µs per transform.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> These radices were 54 % of the statements of the stage kernels and are rarely needed. The image shrinks from 50 MB to 32 MB. Making 11 and 13 lazy as well would save 4 MB more; it was not done, because lengths with those factors are common. The list is the preference <code>lazy_radices</code>; the trimmed C library sets it to empty, because <code>juliac</code> compiles nothing at run time.</p><h2 id="Run-time-pass-plans-for-trimmed-programs" tabindex="-1">Run-time pass plans for trimmed programs <a class="header-anchor" href="#Run-time-pass-plans-for-trimmed-programs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Run-time pass plans for trimmed programs {#Run-time-pass-plans-for-trimmed-programs}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-10).</strong> A pass plan (<code>plan_pass</code>, <code>plan_r2c_pass</code>, <code>plan_c2r_pass</code>) built with <code>_runtime</code> (<code>plan_pass_runtime</code>, <code>plan_r2c_pass_runtime</code>, <code>plan_c2r_pass_runtime</code>) uses run-time kernels only: RChain, Bluestein and composite transforms over RChain, the four-step algorithm over RChain. Its type depends on the element type and the number of dimensions, not on the length. The specialised plans are unchanged.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> A library that holds passes of many lengths in one container (as NFFT.jl does for its boxes) has a container of an abstract or length-dependent type, and <code>juliac --trim</code> cannot resolve the calls through it. The first attempt was a keyword <code>runtime = true</code>; the verifier then still checks the specialised branch, whose kernel types depend on the length, so the separate functions were needed. A probe library compiled with <code>juliac --trim=safe</code> holds run-time pass plans in a vector, applies them, and matches the direct DFT to 3·10⁻¹⁴ when called from C. Planning on 64 × 64 × 64 views is 1.8 µs for <code>d = 1</code> and 0.9 µs for <code>d = 2</code> on the M1 (the specialised plan: 4.7 µs and 2.8 µs). Applying the specialised and the run-time plans is within noise of each other on the same views.</p><p><strong>Costs.</strong> The run-time kernels dispatch per stage, which costs a little on short lines (the reason the specialised plans stay the default). For real lengths with large prime factors, the run-time kernel is a Bluestein transform, without the composite split of the specialised plan.</p><h2 id="The-interface:-FFTW.jl&#39;s-names,-FFTL&#39;s-own-functions,-AbstractFFTs-as-a-backend" tabindex="-1">The interface: FFTW.jl&#39;s names, FFTL&#39;s own functions, AbstractFFTs as a backend <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-interface:-FFTW.jl&#39;s-names,-FFTL&#39;s-own-functions,-AbstractFFTs-as-a-backend" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The interface: FFTW.jl&#39;s names, FFTL&#39;s own functions, AbstractFFTs as a backend {#The-interface:-FFTW.jl&#39;s-names,-FFTL&#39;s-own-functions,-AbstractFFTs-as-a-backend}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-06).</strong> FFTL exports FFTW.jl&#39;s names (<code>fft</code>, <code>bfft</code>, <code>ifft</code>, <code>rfft</code>, <code>brfft</code>, <code>irfft</code>, the <code>plan_</code> and in-place forms, the <code>dct</code> family, and the AbstractFFTs helpers), so <code>using FFTL</code> can take the place of <code>using FFTW</code>. <code>r2r</code>, the kinds, the flags and <code>set_num_threads</code> stay public with the prefix, as in FFTW.jl. The DFT and DCT functions are FFTL&#39;s own, not AbstractFFTs&#39;s; the package only does <code>import AbstractFFTs</code>, never <code>using AbstractFFTs</code> or <code>import AbstractFFTs: fft</code>. Generic code that calls <code>AbstractFFTs.fft</code> reaches FFTL through methods of <code>plan_fft</code> and its relatives for <code>AbstractArray</code>s of floating-point numbers (<code>src/abstractffts.jl</code>), which are less specific than FFTW.jl&#39;s <code>StridedArray</code> methods: with FFTW loaded, the generic functions take FFTW for its arrays and FFTL for the rest.</p><p><strong>Why, and what was rejected.</strong></p><ul><li><p>Exported names that are AbstractFFTs&#39;s functions (re-exported bindings) made <code>FFTL.fft === FFTW.fft</code>: any dependency that loads FFTW would take the standard arrays away from code that wrote <code>using FFTL</code>, and the GPU extension&#39;s methods would have landed next to CUDA.jl&#39;s.</p></li><li><p>Methods with FFTW.jl&#39;s exact signatures: the package loaded last overwrites the other for the whole process, and a package depending on both fails to precompile (&quot;Method overwriting is not permitted during Module precompilation&quot;).</p></li><li><p>Methods are global per process, so this is the only arrangement in which a scope with <code>using FFTL</code> reaches FFTL whatever a dependency loads. Tested: with <code>B -&gt; FFTL</code> and <code>B -&gt; A -&gt; FFTW</code>, B gets FFTL and A gets FFTW; a scope that loads both must qualify the name.</p></li></ul><h2 id="Scope" tabindex="-1">Scope <a class="header-anchor" href="#Scope" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scope {#Scope}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-01).</strong> Real transforms (r2c, c2r, all real-to-real kinds including halfcomplex), a KernelAbstractions GPU path (complex; Metal and CUDA tuned), a trimmed C library (<code>libfftl</code>, all CPU transforms) and, from 2026-11-02, generic float types are part of FFTL.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> Real passes are needed by NFFT.jl, and the GPU path reuses the generator and the plan structure. The parts that do not depend on the backend (codelet generation, factorisation, twiddles, pass structure) stay separate from the CPU execution so that the GPU path can share them.</p>',48)])])}const f=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{m as __pageData,f as default};
